Stack and queue
A stack is a pile of plates: last in, first out (LIFO). A queue is a ticket line: first in, first out (FIFO). Every operation should be O(1); the exam often tests whether you know which end to use to keep it O(1).
Stack:  push 1,2,3  ->  [1, 2, 3] <- top     pop() gives 3
Queue:  enqueue 1,2,3 -> front [1, 2, 3] rear  dequeue() gives 1
Stack (Python list)
class Stack:
   def __init__(self):
       self.items = []

   def push(self, x):                   # O(1)
       self.items.append(x)

   def pop(self):                       # O(1)
       if self.is_empty():
           raise IndexError("stack underflow")
       return self.items.pop()

   def peek(self):                      # O(1)
       if self.is_empty():
           raise IndexError("stack is empty")
       return self.items[-1]

   def is_empty(self):
       return len(self.items) == 0

   def size(self):
       return len(self.items)
Stack using a linked list (combo favourite)
Push = add at head, pop = delete head. Both O(1). Never use the tail of a singly list for a stack: delete-tail is O(n).
class LinkedStack:
   def __init__(self):
       self.top = None

   def push(self, x):                   # O(1)
       n = Node(x)
       n.next = self.top
       self.top = n

   def pop(self):                       # O(1)
       if self.top is None:
           raise IndexError("stack underflow")
       val = self.top.data
       self.top = self.top.next
       return val
Queue using a linked list
Enqueue at the rear (add tail with a tail pointer), dequeue at the front (delete head). Both O(1). The classic bug: forgetting to set rear = None when the last item leaves.
class LinkedQueue:
   def __init__(self):
       self.front = self.rear = None

   def enqueue(self, x):                # O(1)
       n = Node(x)
       if self.rear is None:
           self.front = self.rear = n
       else:
           self.rear.next = n
           self.rear = n

   def dequeue(self):                   # O(1)
       if self.front is None:
           raise IndexError("queue underflow")
       val = self.front.data
       self.front = self.front.next
       if self.front is None:           # queue became empty
           self.rear = None
       return val

   def is_empty(self):
       return self.front is None
Shortcut in real Python: from collections import deque, then q.append(x) and q.popleft(), both O(1). Avoid list.pop(0): it shifts every item, so it is O(n).
Circular queue (fixed-size array)
The rear wraps to index 0 when it passes the end, so freed slots get reused. The key line is % self.cap.
class CircularQueue:
   def __init__(self, k):
       self.q = [None] * k
       self.cap = k
       self.front = 0
       self.size = 0

   def enqueue(self, x):                # O(1)
       if self.size == self.cap:
           raise OverflowError("queue full")
       rear = (self.front + self.size) % self.cap
       self.q[rear] = x
       self.size += 1

   def dequeue(self):                   # O(1)
       if self.size == 0:
           raise IndexError("queue empty")
       x = self.q[self.front]
       self.front = (self.front + 1) % self.cap
       self.size -= 1
       return x
Queue using two stacks
Push into inbox. To dequeue, pour inbox into outbox only when outbox is empty; pouring reverses the order, so the oldest item ends on top. Each item moves at most twice, so it is amortized O(1).
class QueueTwoStacks:
   def __init__(self):
       self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []

   def enqueue(self, x):
       self.inbox.append(x)

   def dequeue(self):
       if not self.outbox:              # refill only when empty
           while self.inbox:
               self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())
       if not self.outbox:
           raise IndexError("queue empty")
       return self.outbox.pop()
