Sorting
Three slow-but-simple sorts (O(n²)) and three fast ones (O(n log n)). Learn each with its one-line hook, then its trace on [5, 2, 4, 1, 3]; if you can reproduce the trace, you can reproduce the code.
Bubble sort — "the biggest bubbles to the end each pass"
Compare neighbours, swap if out of order. After pass i, the last i items are final. The swapped flag stops early on sorted input, which gives the O(n) best case.
def bubble_sort(a):
   n = len(a)
   for i in range(n - 1):
       swapped = False
       for j in range(n - 1 - i):       # last i items already in place
           if a[j] > a[j + 1]:
               a[j], a[j + 1] = a[j + 1], a[j]
               swapped = True
       if not swapped:                  # no swaps -> already sorted
           break
   return a
 
pass 1: [2, 4, 1, 3, 5]   5 bubbled to the end
pass 2: [2, 1, 3, 4, 5]
pass 3: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
pass 4: no swaps -> stop
Selection sort — "select the minimum, swap it to the front"
Always n²/2 comparisons, even on sorted input, but only n − 1 swaps (useful when swaps are expensive). Not stable.
def selection_sort(a):
   n = len(a)
   for i in range(n - 1):
       m = i
       for j in range(i + 1, n):
           if a[j] < a[m]:
               m = j
       a[i], a[m] = a[m], a[i]
   return a
 
pass 1: [1, 2, 4, 5, 3]   min 1 swapped to index 0
pass 2: [1, 2, 4, 5, 3]   2 already in place
pass 3: [1, 2, 3, 5, 4]
pass 4: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Insertion sort — "pick up a card, shift bigger cards right, drop it in the gap"
The left part is always sorted. Best on nearly sorted data: O(n) when already sorted.
def insertion_sort(a):
   for i in range(1, len(a)):
       key = a[i]
       j = i - 1
       while j >= 0 and a[j] > key:     # shift bigger ones right
           a[j + 1] = a[j]
           j -= 1
       a[j + 1] = key                   # drop the card in the gap
   return a
 
