Complexity in one glance
You don't need to memorize complexities if you can derive them in ten seconds. Use these rules, then check against the summary below.
Time rules:
• One loop over n items → O(n). A loop inside a loop over n → O(n²).
• The range halves each step (binary search, balanced BST) → O(log n).
• Split in half, then O(n) work to combine (merge sort) → log n levels × n work = O(n log n).
• Two pointers moving through a list once → still O(n), even with two pointers.
• Amortized O(1): an occasional slow step paid for by many fast ones (two-stack queue, list append).
Space rules:
• Only a few variables or pointers → O(1).
• A set, stack, or new list holding up to n items → O(n).
• Recursion costs its depth in stack space: recursive binary search O(log n), BST recursion O(h), quick sort O(log n) average.
