Assignment answers (Q1–Q10)
Each answer uses the Node and SLL classes from the linked lists section. For every one, learn the idea line first; the code follows from it.
Q1. Singly linked list class
The full SLL class in the linked lists section is the answer (add/delete at head and tail, add/delete after, search).
Q2. Common elements of two lists
Idea: put list 1's values in a set, walk list 2, keep values found in the set. Build the result with a dummy node + tail so each append is O(1).
def common_elements(l1, l2):             # O(m + n) time, O(m) space
   seen = set()
   cur = l1.head
   while cur:
       seen.add(cur.data)
       cur = cur.next
   dummy = Node(None)                   # dummy-node trick for building lists
   tail = dummy
   cur = l2.head
   while cur:
       if cur.data in seen:
           tail.next = Node(cur.data)
           tail = tail.next
       cur = cur.next
   result = SLL()
   result.head = dummy.next
   return result
If both lists are sorted (likely twist), use merge-style two pointers: O(m + n) time, O(1) extra.
def common_sorted(l1, l2):
   a, b = l1.head, l2.head
   dummy = tail = Node(None)
   while a and b:
       if a.data == b.data:
           tail.next = Node(a.data); tail = tail.next
           a, b = a.next, b.next
       elif a.data < b.data:
           a = a.next
       else:
           b = b.next
   result = SLL()
   result.head = dummy.next
   return result
No-extra-space answer for unsorted lists: nested loops, O(m × n) time, O(1) space. Mention it as the trade-off.
Q3. Sum of the last n nodes in one pass
Idea: a sliding window. Add every node; once more than n nodes are inside, subtract the oldest using a lagging pointer.
def sum_last_n(ll, n):                   # O(L) time, O(1) space
   window = 0
   count = 0
   lag = ll.head
   cur = ll.head
   while cur:
       window += cur.data
       count += 1
       if count > n:                    # window too big -> drop oldest
           window -= lag.data
           lag = lag.next
       cur = cur.next
   return window                        # if n > length, gives total sum
 
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n = 3
add 1, 2, 3        -> window = 6
add 4, drop 1      -> window = 9
add 5, drop 2      -> window = 12   answer: 3 + 4 + 5 = 12
Q4. Reverse a singly linked list
Idea: walk once and flip every arrow backwards. Hook: save, flip, step, step.
def reverse(self):                       # method of SLL: O(n) time, O(1) space
   prev, cur = None, self.head
   while cur:
       nxt = cur.next                   # save
       cur.next = prev                  # flip
       prev = cur                       # step prev
       cur = nxt                        # step cur
   self.head = prev

def reverse_nodes(head):                 # standalone version, returns new head
   prev = None
   while head:
       nxt = head.next
       head.next = prev
       prev = head
       head = nxt
   return prev
 
start:   None   1 -> 2 -> 3 -> None
step 1:  None <- 1    2 -> 3 -> None      (prev=1, cur=2)
step 2:  None <- 1 <- 2    3 -> None      (prev=2, cur=3)
step 3:  None <- 1 <- 2 <- 3              (prev=3, cur=None) -> head = 3
Stack version (combo): push every value, then pop them back into the nodes. O(n) space, so the pointer version is better.
Q5. Split into two lists of alternate nodes
Idea: two dummy-node lists; send nodes to A, B, A, B… by flipping a flag. This reuses the original nodes, so the original list is consumed.
def split_alternate(ll):                 # O(n) time, O(1) extra space
   da, db = Node(None), Node(None)
   ta, tb = da, db
   cur = ll.head
   turn_a = True
   while cur:
       if turn_a:
           ta.next = cur; ta = cur
       else:
           tb.next = cur; tb = cur
       turn_a = not turn_a
       cur = cur.next
   ta.next = None                       # cut the old links
   tb.next = None
   a, b = SLL(), SLL()
   a.head, b.head = da.next, db.next
   ll.head = None
   return a, b
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5] → [1, 3, 5] and [2, 4]. If the original must survive, create Node(cur.data) copies instead (O(n) extra space).
Q6. Is the list a palindrome?
Easy idea (stack): push all values; popping gives them in reverse; compare with a forward walk.
def is_palindrome_stack(ll):             # O(n) time, O(n) space
   stack = []
   cur = ll.head
   while cur:
       stack.append(cur.data)
       cur = cur.next
   cur = ll.head
   while cur:
       if cur.data != stack.pop():      # stack gives values in reverse
           return False
       cur = cur.next
   return True
Best idea (O(1) space): find the middle with slow/fast pointers, reverse the second half, compare the halves, then reverse back. This combines Q8 + Q4.
def is_palindrome(ll):                   # O(n) time, O(1) space
   head = ll.head
   if head is None or head.next is None:
       return True
   slow = fast = head                   # 1. find end of first half
   while fast.next and fast.next.next:
       slow = slow.next
       fast = fast.next.next
   second = reverse_nodes(slow.next)    # 2. reverse second half
   p1, p2, ok = head, second, True
   while p2:                            # 3. compare
       if p1.data != p2.data:
           ok = False
           break
       p1, p2 = p1.next, p2.next
   slow.next = reverse_nodes(second)    # 4. restore the list
   return ok
Q7. Remove duplicates efficiently
Unsorted list: remember what you've seen in a set; unlink a node if seen before. Keep prev so you can unlink.
def remove_duplicates(ll):               # O(n) time, O(n) space
   seen = set()
   prev, cur = None, ll.head
   while cur:
       if cur.data in seen:
           prev.next = cur.next         # unlink cur
       else:
           seen.add(cur.data)
           prev = cur
       cur = cur.next
Sorted list: duplicates sit next to each other, so compare with the next node. No extra space.
def remove_duplicates_sorted(ll):        # O(n) time, O(1) space
   cur = ll.head
   while cur and cur.next:
       if cur.data == cur.next.data:
           cur.next = cur.next.next     # don't move cur: may be more dups
       else:
           cur = cur.next
[3, 1, 3, 2, 1, 3] → [3, 1, 2]. The no-extra-space unsorted answer is nested loops, O(n²).
Q8. Middle element without iterating all elements
Idea: tortoise and hare. Slow moves 1, fast moves 2; when fast hits the end, slow is in the middle. The loop runs only about n/2 times.
def middle(ll):                          # O(n) time, O(1) space
   slow = fast = ll.head
   while fast and fast.next:
       slow = slow.next                 # 1 step
       fast = fast.next.next            # 2 steps
   return slow.data if slow else None   # even length -> second middle
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5] → 3. [1, 2, 3, 4] → 3 (second middle). For the first middle (2), loop on while fast.next and fast.next.next.
Q9. Remove odd elements from a circular singly linked list
Idea: check every node after the head and skip the odd ones; when the loop returns to the head, cur is the last node, so finally check the head itself and re-link last → new head. Checking the head last avoids breaking the loop's stop condition.
def remove_odd_circular(cll):            # O(n) time, O(1) space
   if cll.head is None:
       return
   cur = cll.head
   while cur.next is not cll.head:      # 1. check every node AFTER head
       if cur.next.data % 2 != 0:
           cur.next = cur.next.next
       else:
           cur = cur.next
   if cll.head.data % 2 != 0:           # 2. finally check head (cur = last now)
       if cll.head.next is cll.head:    # head was the only node
           cll.head = None
       else:
           cur.next = cll.head.next
           cll.head = cll.head.next
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] → [2, 4, 6], still circular. [1, 3, 5] → empty. This reads "odd elements" as odd values; if your teacher means odd positions, use a position counter instead of % 2 on the data.
Q10. Does the list contain a cycle?
Idea (Floyd): two runners on a track. If the track loops, the fast runner (2 steps) eventually laps the slow one (1 step). If there's no loop, fast reaches None.
def has_cycle(head):                     # O(n) time, O(1) space
   slow = fast = head
   while fast and fast.next:
       slow = slow.next
       fast = fast.next.next
       if slow is fast:                 # fast caught up inside a loop
           return True
   return False
Bonus follow-up ("where does the cycle start?"): after they meet, move one pointer back to head and step both by 1; they meet at the start.
def cycle_start(head):
   slow = fast = head
   while fast and fast.next:
       slow = slow.next
       fast = fast.next.next
       if slow is fast:
           slow = head                  # restart one pointer at head
           while slow is not fast:
               slow = slow.next
               fast = fast.next
           return slow
   return None
Simple alternative: store visited nodes in a set; a repeat means a cycle. O(n) space. Use is (same node), not == (same value).
